      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.085,
            0.481,
            0.103
          ],
          "content": "**Applying Ohm’s law [Eq. (11.5)], we get**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.108,
            0.527,
            0.125
          ],
          "content": "H = I ^ { 2 }   R t\\tag{11.21}",
          "image_source": "images/page_18_equation_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.126,
            0.531,
            0.31
          ],
          "content": "**This is known as Joule’s law of heating. The law implies that heat produced in a resistor is (i) directly proportional to the square of current for a given resistance, (ii) directly proportional to resistance for a given current, and (iii) directly proportional to the time for which the current flows through the resistor. In practical situations, when an electric appliance is connected to a known voltage source, Eq. (11.21) is used after calculating the current through it, using the** relation $I   = V / R .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.545,
            0.115,
            0.922,
            0.271
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.552,
                0.278,
                0.671,
                0.293
              ],
              "content": "Figure 11.13"
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.551,
                0.292,
                0.924,
                0.308
              ],
              "content": "A steady current in a purely resistive electric circuit"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_18_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.328,
            0.29,
            0.344
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Example 11.10"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.344,
            0.723,
            0.41
          ],
          "content": "**An electric iron consumes energy at a rate of 840 W when heating is at the maximum rate and 360 W when the heating is at the minimum. The voltage is 220 V. What are the current and the resistance in each case?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.426,
            0.224,
            0.441
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Solution"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.44,
            0.58,
            0.629
          ],
          "sub_type": "code",
          "content": "```txt\nFrom Eq. (11.19), we know that the power input is\nP = V I\nThus the current I = P/V\n(a) When heating is at the maximum rate,\nI = 840 W/220 V = 3.82 A;\nand the resistance of the electric iron is\nR = V/I = 220 V/3.82 A = 57.60 Ω.\n(b) When heating is at the minimum rate,\nI = 360 W/220 V = 1.64 A;\nand the resistance of the electric iron is\nR = V/I = 220 V/1.64 A = 134.15 Ω.\n```",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": []
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.646,
            0.288,
            0.662
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Example 11.11"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.662,
            0.721,
            0.697
          ],
          "content": "**100 J of heat is produced each second in a 4** Ω **resistance. Find the potential difference across the resistor.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.705,
            0.714,
            0.876
          ],
          "sub_type": "code",
          "content": "```txt\nSolution\nH = 100 J, R = 4 Ω, t = 1 s, V = ?\nFrom Eq. (11.21) we have the current through the resistor as\nI √(H/Rt)\n= √[100 J/(4 Ω × 1 s)]\n= 5 A\nThus the potential difference across the resistor, V [from Eq. (11.5)] is\nV = IR\n= 5 A × 4 Ω\n= 20 V.\n```",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": []
        },
        {
